
  [1mSection 1 — Architecture and modules[0m

  The organizing idea: [1mnotifications are derived, never stored.[0m A notification is not a record the app creates and files
  away; it's a pure function of (tasks, the current time, what you've already dismissed). That kills a whole class of
  bugs — a notification log can drift out of sync with the tasks it describes, and reconciling the two is exactly the
  kind of work that makes notification systems miserable.

  index.html
    src/clock.js          now() + tick subscription       (injectable; tests never sleep)
    src/storage.js        localStorage load/save          (the persistence seam)
    src/tasks.js          task record + CRUD, pure
    src/notifications.js  computeNotifications(tasks, now, acks) -> Notification[]
    src/ui/task-list.js   renders tasks, watch toggle
    src/ui/bell.js        renders bell + badge + panel
    src/app.js            wiring: state, recompute triggers, render

  [38;5;153mnotifications.js[39m is the deliverable and it has no DOM and no clock of its own — time arrives as an argument. That's
  what makes the whole engine testable without a browser, and it's why the OS-notification layer can be added later as
  another consumer of the same output.

  [38;5;153mstorage.js[39m exists as a seam rather than scattered [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m calls, so the "add a backend later" door you left open
  stays cheap to walk through.

  [1mTooling[0m — nothing is configured in this repo yet, and it's cheapest to decide now. My recommendation is the first two:

  - [1mESLint + Prettier[0m — standard, catches real mistakes in untyped JS.
  - [1mVitest[0m — the engine is pure functions; unit tests are the natural fit and give TDD something to bite on.
  - [1mPlaywright (e2e)[0m — proves the bell actually updates in a browser. Worth it only if you want that guarantee; it's
    real setup cost for a small app.
  - [1mFuzz/mutation testing[0m — I'd skip it. The engine's input space is small enough to cover with explicit cases.

  Does this structure look right, and which tooling do you want?

